# Narration script for the BeeGFS video. Each scene = list of lines; each line = one visual beat.
# Written to be spoken. Numbers spelled out and letters spaced for the TTS voice.
SCRIPT = [
("s0", [
 "We've looked at G P F S, which spreads its intelligence across every node, and Lustre, which separates metadata from data and gives each disk a single owner. Now the third of the big three: Bee G F S, whose makers describe it as built for performance and ease of use.",
 "Its design fits on a postcard. Four kinds of service. A management service that keeps a registry. Metadata servers that know where things are. Storage servers that hold the data. And a client that mounts it all as an ordinary directory.",
 "Watch a file open. The client asks a metadata server about the file, learns how it's striped, and then streams data to and from the storage servers directly, in parallel.",
 "We'll cover where it came from, how files are striped and how metadata is spread out, what it promises about consistency, the network layer, mirroring, a burst buffer tool called Beyond, and how it compares with the other two. And I'll flag the places where I couldn't verify a claim.",
]),
("s1", [
 "Bee G F S began in two thousand five, as an in-house project at the Fraunhofer Institute for Industrial Mathematics in Germany, to replace the file system on the institute's new compute cluster. Its original name was F h G F S, for Fraunhofer Gesellschaft File System.",
 "The first beta was announced at I S C oh seven in Dresden, and shown at S C oh seven in Reno. The first stable major release followed about a year later.",
 "In two thousand fourteen, a spin-off company, Think Park Q, was founded to maintain it and sell support, and the software was renamed Bee G F S. Development continues in cooperation with Fraunhofer. By then about a hundred customers used it with commercial support, among them universities, research labs, and companies in finance and oil and gas.",
 "One difference from the other two stories. I could not find a single founding paper for Bee G F S, comparable to the two thousand two papers for G P F S and Lustre. The documentation is the primary source here, alongside trade press interviews, which are mostly vendor voices. I'll say which is which.",
 "Licensing is different too. The client is G P L, version two. The servers are proprietary, but a Community Edition is free to use, and the Enterprise Edition adds features and requires a support subscription. We'll see exactly which features in a few minutes, because it matters.",
]),
("s2", [
 "Start with what Bee G F S is built on. The servers are ordinary user-space programs, not kernel modules, running on top of a local file system on each target. Metadata usually sits on ext four, and storage usually on X F S, according to the documentation and a partner's guide.",
 "The client is the only piece in the kernel: a patchless module that's compiled for your running kernel, with no reboot needed to install or update it.",
 "Any combination of services can share a machine, and you can run several instances on one. The company's line is that you can start with two servers and add components on the fly. That's a vendor claim, though it matches the design.",
 "Compare the philosophies. G P F S brings its own software RAID and a token-based lock manager. Lustre brings a distributed lock manager and its own network layer. Bee G F S leans on the local file system, and on whatever RAID or Z F S you put underneath. A partner company called it the most lightweight of the three, which is also a vendor-side opinion.",
 "Thin has a price, and we'll find it: fewer documented guarantees, and some protection features sitting in the paid edition.",
]),
("s3", [
 "The management service is the registry and the watchdog. It keeps a list of every other service and its state. It stores no user data, isn't in the I O path, and isn't performance critical. One vendor measured its entire database at eighty eight kilobytes.",
 "Metadata services store the namespace: directory information, ownership and permissions, and the stripe pattern of each file. Each instance has exactly one metadata target, a local file system, typically ext four on mirrored flash.",
 "Storage services hold the file contents as chunk files. A storage target is typically a hardware RAID six, eight plus two or ten plus two, or Z F S RAID Z two. Adding storage servers adds both capacity and bandwidth.",
 "The client mounts the file system, by default at slash mnt slash beegfs, and asks the management service for the topology. From then on it talks straight to the metadata and storage servers. Once a file is open, the metadata server takes no part in reads or writes until it's closed.",
 "Here's an open. The client asks the metadata server, gets the stripe pattern, then sends I O in parallel to the storage servers. It's the same shape as Lustre. The differences are in what each piece does, and that's the next few scenes.",
 "There's also an optional monitoring service that feeds a time series database for dashboards, and older documentation describes an optional admin G U I service. Neither is on the data path.",
]),
("s4", [
 "Files are striped across storage targets, in chunks. One documentation page gives the default chunk size as five hundred twelve kilobytes, though an example output elsewhere in the same docs shows one megabyte, so check your own installation. The simplest pattern, called RAID zero, is plain striping: chunk one to the first target, chunk two to the second, and so on around.",
 "Each target stores a file's share as a chunk file, one per file per target. Chunk files are created only when something is written, and they aren't preallocated. A one byte write makes a one byte chunk file.",
 "You set the pattern per directory: how many targets, and what chunk size. In version eight the command is beegfs entry set. New files inherit the pattern. Existing files don't change; to restripe them you recreate them, or migrate them.",
 "How are targets chosen? By default, randomly. Targets are also labeled normal, low, or emergency, according to free space, and the chooser prefers normal ones. The thresholds are set on the management service, and I couldn't find their default values.",
 "Random choice should sound like the balance problem from the Lustre video. So here is my toy model again: one hundred targets, thousands of files with a heavy tail of sizes, four stripes each, and no free-space steering. The fullest target ends up about one point nine times the average. The capacity labels exist to push back against exactly that.",
 "One practical tip from the docs. Keep a chunk within the client's R D M A buffer size, so messages aren't split. And a chunk size of a few megabytes can cut the message count and improve performance, even on a single target.",
]),
("s5", [
 "Now the part Bee G F S does differently from Lustre. Metadata is distributed per directory. Each metadata server owns an exclusive part of the namespace, and when a directory is created it's assigned to a metadata server, by default at random. The docs say each directory's contents live on one metadata node.",
 "So a tree of directories fans out across all the metadata servers from the start, with no extra feature to switch on, unlike Lustre's D N E. And the documentation says more metadata servers can be added later.",
 "The flip side is my inference, not a documented fact. Because one directory's contents live on one server, a single enormous, busy directory is served by a single metadata server. Spreading files over many directories is the natural remedy, and the docs advise it anyway for very large directories.",
 "How is metadata stored? As extended attributes on a zero byte file in the local file system, one such file per user file. With large inodes, the attributes fit inside the inode itself, so one disk read finds everything. That's why the docs say to format ext four with five hundred twelve byte inodes, or one thousand twenty four if you stripe over more than four targets or use A C L's.",
 "A directory costs two directories and two metadata files, and a file costs one metadata file. Metadata grows linearly with the file count, and there's no central database to corrupt. The sizing rule of thumb: about point three to point five percent of total capacity, or roughly a hundred fifty million files per five hundred gigabytes.",
 "The docs say ext four handles small file workloads significantly faster than other Linux file systems. But X F S allocates inodes dynamically, which helps if you're worried about running out.",
 "Compare the three. G P F S spreads the work with a metanode per file and token managers. Lustre scales metadata by adding targets and sharding directories. Bee G F S shards by directory from day one. Three different answers to the same bottleneck.",
]),
("s6", [
 "What does Bee G F S promise when two clients touch the same file? This is where I have to be careful, because I could not find a clear statement of locking and consistency semantics in the documentation I read. The vendor says it's POSIX compatible. What the docs do describe is caching.",
 "Metadata is cached on the client only briefly by default: milliseconds, controlled by three timeout settings. That means a stat on one client can return slightly old information about a change made on another.",
 "Data caching is its own setting. One client option chooses the file cache type, and another controls syncing when a file closes. A third decides whether fsync commits to the server's disks, or only to the server's cache. Switching that off cuts disk seeks, and trades safety for speed.",
 "Version eight point four adds an experimental feature: remote invalidation. The metadata server remembers which clients cached which inodes, and tells them when something changes. It's off by default, and tracks up to fifty million inodes, about six gibibytes of server memory.",
 "The documentation is candid about the trade-offs. Changes can still become visible late. A race can lose an invalidation, leaving stale metadata for an unbounded, though self-healing, time. Create-heavy workloads see little benefit. And applications that use modification time or size as an instant signal shouldn't rely on it.",
 "My advice, as a skeptic: if your application depends on cross-node locking or instant visibility, test it on your own system. The documentation I read doesn't settle the question.",
]),
("s7", [
 "For the network, Bee G F S uses the OpenFabrics verbs interface, so it supports InfiniBand, RoCE, and Omni-Path, with R D M A, and plain T C P otherwise. Management traffic still travels over T C P.",
 "Clients connect to metadata and storage servers. Metadata servers also connect to each other and to storage servers. Storage servers don't connect to other services. That's a tidy topology, and it means you can count the connections.",
 "Here's the documented formula. Each node type's maximum number of peers is, for a metadata server, the clients, plus two times the other metadata servers, plus the storage servers. For a storage server, the clients plus the metadata servers. For a client, metadata plus storage. A made-up example: a thousand clients, four metadata servers, and twenty storage servers gives up to a thousand twenty six peers per metadata server, and a thousand and four per storage server.",
 "Each connection has R D M A buffers: by default seventy buffers of eight kilobytes, about five hundred sixty kilobytes. The docs say the total should be at least one chunk, and the default chunk is five hundred twelve, which is presumably why. If each pair used one connection, my example works out to about one point two gigabytes of pinned memory per metadata server. The docs warn that R D M A memory can't be swapped out.",
 "Multi-rail has been available on the client since version seven point three. Point a configuration file at your I P over InfiniBand devices, and traffic is balanced across them by connection count. Every interface must reach every service, and they can't sit in separate subnets.",
 "A practical hazard: a misconfigured client can quietly fall back to T C P. The docs give a health command that shows which connections are R D M A, so check it after setup.",
]),
("s8", [
 "Now protection. Bee G F S protects data in two layers. Inside a target, you use hardware RAID or Z F S, as with the other systems. Across targets, there's buddy mirroring.",
 "A buddy group is a pair of targets: a primary and a secondary. Data written to the primary is forwarded to the secondary. You pick targets of similar size, on different servers or failure domains. Storage mirroring is set per directory, and mirrored files take twice the space.",
 "Metadata can be mirrored too, and once you enable it the root directory is mirrored by default. If a target drops out and comes back, it usually resyncs from its buddy automatically, or you can start a resync by hand.",
 "Failover isn't instant. The state change spreads to clients over time, by default about thirty seconds, and the docs say it should stay under a minute. During that window, writes can be lost, or a client can read stale data from the secondary.",
 "If a group is already degraded, one more failure can lose data. An fsync error on a secondary may go unnoticed until its next operation. And mirroring is not a backup: it won't bring back a deleted or overwritten file.",
 "Here's the catch I promised. According to the vendor's edition table, mirroring, high availability, quota enforcement, storage pools with tiering, and several other features are Enterprise only. The Community Edition is capped at one thousand terabytes of hard disk plus two hundred of flash, and Beyond, which we'll meet next, is limited to five contributing nodes in both editions.",
 "So how Bee G F S compares with G P F S and Lustre depends on which edition you mean. Check the table against your needs before assuming a feature is there.",
]),
("s9", [
 "Now Beyond, spelled B-e-e-O-N-D, short for Bee G F S On Demand. It takes the local drives of the compute nodes allocated to a job and pools them into a temporary parallel file system, for the length of the job.",
 "You start it with a list of hosts, a directory for raw data on each node, and a mount point. Every listed host becomes both a client and a server. At job end you stop it, and optionally delete the data. It's usually wired into the scheduler's prologue and epilogue scripts, with Slurm and Torque as the documented examples.",
 "Because it lives on local flash, it works as a burst buffer. The vendor says Fujitsu's ABCI supercomputer uses it for a one petabyte, one terabyte per second N V M E burst buffer. That's a vendor claim, relayed by the trade press, so treat it as indicative. Results should be staged out to persistent storage afterwards, and a parallel copy command helps.",
 "Now version eight, the biggest change in years. The management service was rewritten from scratch, in Rust, with an S Q L database. Management traffic moves to g R P C, which allows T L S encryption, and authentication moves to S H A two fifty six.",
 "The old command line tool is replaced by a new one, written in Go, with noun-verb commands, tables by default, and JSON output. The separate helper daemon is gone.",
 "Version eight also adds remote storage targets, which link files and directories to S three compatible object storage, with two new services to move data in parallel. Remote targets are an Enterprise feature.",
 "One warning from the release notes: version eight isn't compatible with older versions, because network and on-disk formats changed. Upgrades need a plan.",
]),
("s10", [
 "Let's put all three side by side. Born: G P F S from IBM research in the early nineteen nineties, Lustre as a research project in nineteen ninety nine, and Bee G F S in two thousand five at Fraunhofer.",
 "Metadata. G P F S spreads it across all nodes with tokens and a metanode per file. Lustre keeps it on metadata servers, and shards with D N E. Bee G F S spreads directories across metadata servers from the start.",
 "Disk ownership. G P F S shares disks among nodes. Lustre assigns each target to one server at a time. Bee G F S also keeps each target behind a storage service, and mirrors across targets in pairs.",
 "Protection. G P F S has declustered RAID in software, or replication. Lustre relies on RAID or Z F S under each target, with mirrored layouts available. Bee G F S relies on RAID or Z F S, plus buddy mirroring in the Enterprise Edition.",
 "Consistency. G P F S documents single-node-equivalent semantics through tokens. Lustre does it with a distributed lock manager. For Bee G F S I couldn't verify the details from the documentation I read. That's the honest gap in this series, and the first thing I'd test.",
 "A caution about the whole series. Three systems are not all of them. Others, such as Weka, D A O S, Ceph F S, and PanFS, take different approaches, and I haven't covered them. And no fair same-hardware benchmark of these three came up in my research, so none of this tells you which is fastest.",
 "To recap Bee G F S. Four services. Chunked striping, set per directory. Metadata sharded by directory. R D M A networking. Buddy mirroring and many protection features in the paid edition. And Beyond for job-local file systems. The sources are on screen, and the code is in the package.",
]),
]
